# The Archive — environment configuration (ALL OPTIONAL)
#
# Copy to `.env.local` to override. By default the web app (Track A) makes NO
# network calls: leaving everything below unset/blank keeps the local demo
# analyzer (mock) in place, which is the normal, supported default.

# VITE_API_BASE — base URL of the optional Vercel serverless API (Phase 2A).
# Leave blank/unset for the default mock-only behavior (no network, no Fetch
# button in the reference step). When set, the reference step can read a pasted
# product page's declared metadata via the Vercel route `api/product-meta`
# (Phase 3, see api/product-meta.ts) and the analyzer seam selects the backend
# path (a stub until Phase 4). Use your deployment origin so the call is
# same-origin; e2e prefill runs under `vercel dev` or a deploy, not `npm run dev`.
# Example: VITE_API_BASE=https://your-app.vercel.app
VITE_API_BASE=

# VITE_ANALYZER — optional analyzer opt-in (Phase 4). Leave unset (default) to
# keep the LOCAL mock analyzer: no photo is sent anywhere. Set to "vision" AND
# configure VITE_API_BASE to route uploads through the backend vision provider
# (api/analyze.ts). When on, the upload scan copy honestly states the photo is
# sent to a server.
# VITE_ANALYZER=vision

# VITE_CANDIDATES — optional reference-candidate source opt-in (Phase C). Leave
# unset (default) to keep the LOCAL mock candidates: no network. Set to "search"
# AND configure VITE_API_BASE to use the live shopping search (eBay Browse) via
# the Vercel route api/candidate-search.ts. A search failure or 0 results falls
# back to the mock, and manual URL entry is always available. The candidate shape
# is unchanged, so the approval/archive flow is untouched.
# VITE_CANDIDATES=search

# --- Server-only (NOT exposed to the browser — no VITE_ prefix) -------------
# Set these in your Vercel project settings, never in client code.
#
# Used by ALL three api/ routes (see api/_lib/http.ts):
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS — comma-separated list of browser origins allowed to call the
#   API, e.g. https://your-app.vercel.app,http://localhost:5173. Leave unset
#   (default) to allow the deployment's OWN origin only, which is the right
#   setting when the web app and the API are deployed together. There is no
#   wildcard option on purpose: these routes have no auth, so `*` would let any
#   page on the internet spend your eBay and Anthropic quota. A request from a
#   disallowed origin is rejected with 403 before any upstream call is made.
#   Entries must be full origins (scheme + host + optional port), no paths.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=
#
# RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE — optional override for the per-caller request throttle,
#   applied to every route (defaults: analyze 10/min, candidate-search 20/min,
#   product-meta 20/min). Counters are per serverless isolate, so this is a
#   speed bump against a runaway client, not a global guarantee — see the note
#   in api/_lib/http.ts. Raise it if legitimate use hits 429s.
# RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=
#
# Used by api/analyze.ts when VITE_ANALYZER=vision:
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY — your Anthropic API key (required for the vision analyzer).
# ANALYZE_MODEL — optional model override (default: claude-opus-4-8). Set e.g.
#   claude-haiku-4-5 for a cheaper classification.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANALYZE_MODEL=
#
# Used by api/candidate-search.ts when VITE_CANDIDATES=search (Phase C3):
# EBAY_CLIENT_ID / EBAY_CLIENT_SECRET — your eBay developer app credentials. The
#   function exchanges them for a client-credentials OAuth token and queries the
#   eBay Browse API; with either unset the endpoint reports "not configured" and
#   the app stays on the local mock candidates.
# EBAY_CLIENT_ID=
# EBAY_CLIENT_SECRET=
